{"builder":"Add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated input on stdin gives the same JSON array that CSV input gives for the same table, and document it.\n\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. src/parse.js already exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`, so it can split on any separator.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check process.argv for `--tsv`. If it is there, call `parse(input, '\\t')`. If not, keep calling `parse(input)` (comma). Read stdin and print output exactly as the script does now (`JSON.stringify(...)` plus a newline), so runs without the flag behave the same as before.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` through execFileSync with input like 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'. Assert that its stdout equals the stdout of `node bin/csv2json.js` given the same table as CSV ('a,b\\n1,2\\n'). Keep the existing tests.\n4. In README.md, document the --tsv flag: give a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and one sentence saying it reads tab-separated input and prints the same JSON array as CSV.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes. Then open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Run every command from the repository root at the PR head.\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\tOslo\\nbob\\t41\\tRome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv\n  printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,Oslo\\nbob,41,Rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\n  bash -c \"diff <(printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\tOslo\\nbob\\t41\\tRome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv) <(printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,Oslo\\nbob,41,Rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js) && echo SAME\"\nPASS: the --tsv run reads the tab-separated table from stdin and prints a JSON array that is the same as the array the plain CSV run prints for the same table (here, two objects keyed name/age/city with the values ann/30/Oslo and bob/41/Rome).\nFAIL: the --tsv run errors, ignores the tabs (for example, it prints one key per whole line), or prints a JSON array that differs from the CSV run's array.\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun:\n  grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md\n  cat README.md\nPASS: README.md at the head mentions the --tsv flag and explains it (for example, it reads tab-separated input).\nFAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or mentions it without saying what it does.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Run `npm test` and note whether it passes and whether a test covers --tsv.\n- Check that output without the flag is unchanged: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` should still print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}].\n- Try a TSV cell that contains a comma, e.g. `printf 'a\\tb\\n1,5\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv`. It should print [{\"a\":\"1,5\",\"b\":\"2\"}]."}